ULT initiation: SU > 7 mg/dL; SU target: ≤5mg/dL
Febuxostat: first-line, maximum of 40 mg/day;
Allopurinol: second-line, maximum of 200 mg/day in CKD stage 3-4, contraindicated in CKD stage 5;
Pegloticase: when conventional therapy are inadequate;
For gout flare:
Short-term glucocorticoids are recommended;
Colchicine or NSAIDs should be used with caution;
IL-1 inhibitors can be considered as an alternative

For gout flare:
Colchicine is preferred;
NSAIDs should be avoid;
Short-term glucocorticoids and IL-1 inhibitors can be considered as appropriate;
For heart failure:
Appropriate ULT is emphasized;
Angiotensin receptor-neprilysin inhibitors (ARNI) and sodium-glucose co-transporter 2 (SGLT-2) inhibitors are prioritized;
Losartan potassium is prefered for initiation anti-hypertension;
Thiazide diuretics should be minimized

In patients with gastointestinal hemorrhage risk: COX-2 inhibitors are prefered;
In patients with a history peptic ulcer or hemorrhage: colchicine or short-term glucocorticoid is recommended, protectant with PPI or mucosal protectant )
In the case of active hemorrhage: short-term parenteral glucocorticoid or anakinra is recommended as appropriate, along with standard therapy for peptic conditions

Appropriate exercise during arthritis relief is recommended;
Additional weight management is emphasized if overweight or obese;
Initiation of ULT is recommended, with the SU target 3.5 mg/dl;
NSAID is preferred, with course of 7-10 days;
Long-term NSAIDs, exceeding 4 weeks, is not suggested;
Intra-articular glucocorticoids are recommended for hip or knee involvement;
Surgeries can be considerd for refractory pain or disability in large joints

Abbreviations
CKD, chronic kidney disease; ULT, urate-lowering therapy;
SU, serum urate;
NSAID, non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drug;
IL, interleukin; AMI; acute myocardial infarction; COI, cyclooxygenase; PPI, proton pump inhibitor
